Take us away.

00:19:30.330 --> 00:19:32.010
RICH MEESTERS: Thanks, Rick.

00:19:32.010 --> 00:19:33.750
Watch where the
mic goes, I guess.

00:19:33.750 --> 00:19:36.900
So what I want to do is
delve a little bit further

00:19:36.900 --> 00:19:40.380
down into the architectural
components of Citrix Virtual

00:19:40.380 --> 00:19:41.790
Apps and Desktops on GCP.

00:19:45.810 --> 00:19:48.060
Rick has gone through
what Citrix Cloud was,

00:19:48.060 --> 00:19:50.100
what the Workspace is.

00:19:50.100 --> 00:19:55.020
Really it's a bunch of
cloud-delivered services

00:19:55.020 --> 00:19:58.860
that wrap around this
whole Workspace experience.

00:19:58.860 --> 00:20:02.940
A lot of these will come into
play in that Citrix Google

00:20:02.940 --> 00:20:06.540
ecosystem in terms of
delivering an overall service

00:20:06.540 --> 00:20:08.200
to your customers.

00:20:08.200 --> 00:20:11.250
But what we want to focus
on is really those items

00:20:11.250 --> 00:20:13.575
that we're dealing
with in the demo.

00:20:13.575 --> 00:20:17.790
And fundamentally there
what we're looking at

00:20:17.790 --> 00:20:21.590
is what Jeff started building
at the beginning of this session

00:20:21.590 --> 00:20:23.340
and the focus of the
components that we're

00:20:23.340 --> 00:20:26.190
putting on Google Cloud
is really the Virtual Apps

00:20:26.190 --> 00:20:27.270
and Desktop services.

00:20:27.270 --> 00:20:29.340
And then we're going
to front end that

00:20:29.340 --> 00:20:32.940
with the Workspace and
the Workspace experience

00:20:32.940 --> 00:20:35.850
from an end user experience
perspective coming off

00:20:35.850 --> 00:20:36.850
the Chromebook.

00:20:39.430 --> 00:20:44.440
So what is the Citrix Virtual
Apps and Desktop Services?

00:20:44.440 --> 00:20:46.130
I think Rick kind of covered it.

00:20:46.130 --> 00:20:48.310
It's really the
foundation of what

00:20:48.310 --> 00:20:50.740
we've been doing at Citrix
for the last approximately

00:20:50.740 --> 00:20:52.240
30 years.

00:20:52.240 --> 00:20:55.090
And that is deploying
Windows workloads

00:20:55.090 --> 00:20:58.630
and now Linux workloads
in a centralized location,

00:20:58.630 --> 00:21:00.460
whether that's on
a customer premise

00:21:00.460 --> 00:21:04.570
or in a public cloud like
GCP, and then allowing users

00:21:04.570 --> 00:21:08.780
to access those applications
in a secure fashion.

00:21:08.780 --> 00:21:12.370
So the Citrix Virtual
Apps and Desktop service

00:21:12.370 --> 00:21:15.740
itself is really the
cloud-hosted control plane.

00:21:15.740 --> 00:21:17.638
And that's
Citrix-managed component.

00:21:17.638 --> 00:21:19.930
So that's the glue that holds
all of the infrastructure

00:21:19.930 --> 00:21:21.250
together.

00:21:21.250 --> 00:21:24.730
As well as workloads that our
customers put in their data

00:21:24.730 --> 00:21:26.640
centers wherever they
may want to put them.

00:21:26.640 --> 00:21:29.410
So in their data centers in
public cloud, pretty much

00:21:29.410 --> 00:21:31.530
wherever they want to put them.

00:21:31.530 --> 00:21:35.010
And then the user's
access those applications

00:21:35.010 --> 00:21:37.950
using the Workspace
App anywhere, anytime,

00:21:37.950 --> 00:21:41.820
from any device that they
want to access them from.

00:21:41.820 --> 00:21:43.290
The key components
here that will

00:21:43.290 --> 00:21:45.240
start to kind of break
down and talk about,

00:21:45.240 --> 00:21:47.420
the Citrix Connectors.

00:21:47.420 --> 00:21:49.350
They're the component
that provide

00:21:49.350 --> 00:21:54.360
that link between
customers' GCP, resource

00:21:54.360 --> 00:21:58.050
location, and the Citrix
Cloud control plane.

00:21:58.050 --> 00:22:00.450
As well as the VDAs,
which is the agent,

00:22:00.450 --> 00:22:03.270
or what we call a Virtual
Delivery Agent, that we install

00:22:03.270 --> 00:22:07.830
on the virtual desktops
or the Windows Server,

00:22:07.830 --> 00:22:10.020
The Linux Server,
to provide access

00:22:10.020 --> 00:22:13.460
to the applications on it.

00:22:13.460 --> 00:22:16.880
So in terms of what components
we actually require,

00:22:16.880 --> 00:22:20.180
our minimal set is we need
a couple of Citrix Cloud

00:22:20.180 --> 00:22:21.110
Connectors.

00:22:21.110 --> 00:22:24.530
And those are Windows
Server instances.

00:22:24.530 --> 00:22:26.190
And the Virtual Delivery Agents.

00:22:26.190 --> 00:22:28.880
So those agents running
on Windows or Linux

00:22:28.880 --> 00:22:32.530
instances to deliver
the applications.

00:22:32.530 --> 00:22:34.270
Other components
that we require.

00:22:34.270 --> 00:22:36.940
Active Directory is required
for a Citrix system.

00:22:36.940 --> 00:22:41.740
All of our Windows
machines are domain joined.

00:22:41.740 --> 00:22:44.290
And so you absolutely have
to have Active Directory

00:22:44.290 --> 00:22:45.730
in your environment.

00:22:45.730 --> 00:22:48.693
But in many customer situations,
from a security perspective,

00:22:48.693 --> 00:22:50.110
they don't at least
initially want

00:22:50.110 --> 00:22:52.840
to put Active Directory
controllers into a public cloud

00:22:52.840 --> 00:22:54.830
infrastructure.

00:22:54.830 --> 00:22:58.430
So really what we need
is we need the service.

00:22:58.430 --> 00:23:02.540
Other optional components that
we have within the environment.

00:23:02.540 --> 00:23:07.820
We have SMB file shares
for Windows user profiles.

00:23:07.820 --> 00:23:11.390
We have our Citrix
ADCs or gateways.

00:23:11.390 --> 00:23:15.050
And we'll talk a little bit
more about what the ADC does,

00:23:15.050 --> 00:23:18.110
but it's part of our access
to your infrastructure.

00:23:18.110 --> 00:23:19.700
As well as StoreFront.

00:23:19.700 --> 00:23:23.420
We've talked about the Workspace
and Workspace experience.

00:23:23.420 --> 00:23:26.503
Some customers opt to go with
our older version of that,

00:23:26.503 --> 00:23:27.920
which is StoreFront,
and then they

00:23:27.920 --> 00:23:30.530
can build that within
their own infrastructure

00:23:30.530 --> 00:23:32.390
and control the routing
of their traffic

00:23:32.390 --> 00:23:35.330
through those infrastructures.

00:23:35.330 --> 00:23:38.090
And finally, Federated
Authentication

00:23:38.090 --> 00:23:40.460
and Certificate
Services are components

00:23:40.460 --> 00:23:44.570
that allow us to federate
SAML based authentication

00:23:44.570 --> 00:23:46.503
into our Active
Directory components.

00:23:46.503 --> 00:23:48.170
So these are other
additional components

00:23:48.170 --> 00:23:49.837
that we'll talk a
little bit about as we

00:23:49.837 --> 00:23:51.510
go through the
reference architectures.

00:23:54.730 --> 00:23:56.460
So the Cloud
Connectors, as we said,

00:23:56.460 --> 00:23:59.310
they really provide
that glue or that layer

00:23:59.310 --> 00:24:03.930
in between the customer's
environment and the Citrix

00:24:03.930 --> 00:24:05.850
Managed Control plane.

00:24:05.850 --> 00:24:08.130
And they're there
to provide services

00:24:08.130 --> 00:24:12.660
to support the connection
between those two components.

00:24:12.660 --> 00:24:16.410
We do say we want to have two
Connectors in every resource

00:24:16.410 --> 00:24:17.460
location that we have.

00:24:17.460 --> 00:24:20.790
These Connectors are
Citrix-managed components.

00:24:20.790 --> 00:24:24.000
So we will reach down
to those components.

00:24:24.000 --> 00:24:27.330
Or when they reach up to us,
we'll check their version

00:24:27.330 --> 00:24:29.760
and we will update
them periodically.

00:24:29.760 --> 00:24:31.320
So we want to have
a minimum of two

00:24:31.320 --> 00:24:33.880
of those in any
resource location

00:24:33.880 --> 00:24:36.150
so we maintain
high availability.

00:24:36.150 --> 00:24:39.900
We also want to put
these Connectors

00:24:39.900 --> 00:24:43.410
in different availability
zones within GCP

00:24:43.410 --> 00:24:45.120
so that we can
tolerate, again, we

00:24:45.120 --> 00:24:46.590
get higher levels
of availability

00:24:46.590 --> 00:24:51.380
and tolerate potential
failures within a zone.

00:24:51.380 --> 00:24:55.070
Within that component,
there's multiple services

00:24:55.070 --> 00:24:55.880
that are running.

00:24:55.880 --> 00:24:59.660
The Secure Ticket Authority
is used by the gateway

00:24:59.660 --> 00:25:01.400
to ensure that
communications are

00:25:01.400 --> 00:25:06.290
secure between the front end,
user end, and the back end.

00:25:06.290 --> 00:25:09.200
Gateway services, if we're
using the Gateway in the cloud,

00:25:09.200 --> 00:25:14.720
provides that path for the
traffic from the workload

00:25:14.720 --> 00:25:17.630
to the user's end
point to transit.

00:25:17.630 --> 00:25:20.407
Brokering, kind of
its primary function

00:25:20.407 --> 00:25:22.490
is just to make sure that
users are getting access

00:25:22.490 --> 00:25:25.912
to the resources they need, as
well as provisioning services,

00:25:25.912 --> 00:25:28.370
and we'll talk a little bit
about machine creation services

00:25:28.370 --> 00:25:32.030
later, which allow us to do
single instance management

00:25:32.030 --> 00:25:34.020
and managing the identity.

00:25:34.020 --> 00:25:37.130
So Citrix does not maintain
the customer's Active Directory

00:25:37.130 --> 00:25:38.180
in our control plane.

00:25:38.180 --> 00:25:41.900
The customer maintains that on
their own resource location.

00:25:41.900 --> 00:25:49.680
And the identity service within
the Connector will manage that.

00:25:49.680 --> 00:25:51.870
Typically what we see
with these are usually

00:25:51.870 --> 00:25:56.160
4 vCPUs and 12 to
16 gigabytes of RAM.

00:25:56.160 --> 00:25:58.320
And the communication
from the Connector

00:25:58.320 --> 00:26:01.560
is a TLS 1.2 outbound
only connection

00:26:01.560 --> 00:26:02.790
to the Citrix control plane.

00:26:07.640 --> 00:26:09.740
The Windows Virtual
Delivery Agent.

00:26:09.740 --> 00:26:12.440
This is the component that
sits on top of the workloads.

00:26:12.440 --> 00:26:15.080
And it connects the
Windows and Linux instances

00:26:15.080 --> 00:26:17.450
to the Virtual Apps
and Desktop service.

00:26:17.450 --> 00:26:19.790
It's a software package that's
installed on that server

00:26:19.790 --> 00:26:21.770
or desktop instance.

00:26:21.770 --> 00:26:24.110
And it basically communicates
with the Connectors

00:26:24.110 --> 00:26:27.710
on GCP port 80 to register
itself with the system

00:26:27.710 --> 00:26:30.380
and also communicates out
to the end users outbound

00:26:30.380 --> 00:26:34.760
with TCP 1494/2598 natively.

00:26:34.760 --> 00:26:37.340
And often that is done through
a gateway service, where that

00:26:37.340 --> 00:26:45.200
is then encapsulated in 443.

00:26:45.200 --> 00:26:46.880
Now one of the
important points when

00:26:46.880 --> 00:26:48.610
you're looking at
your infrastructure

00:26:48.610 --> 00:26:49.760
is the scalability.

00:26:49.760 --> 00:26:51.890
Most of your
infrastructure that you

00:26:51.890 --> 00:26:54.607
build in a Citrix environment
is going to be workload.

00:26:54.607 --> 00:26:55.940
We have a few Connector servers.

00:26:55.940 --> 00:26:57.860
We'll have some store
front, potentially.

00:26:57.860 --> 00:26:59.813
Some federated
authentication services.

00:26:59.813 --> 00:27:01.730
But you're really only
talking about a handful

00:27:01.730 --> 00:27:03.890
of servers at that point.

00:27:03.890 --> 00:27:06.410
So your majority is going to
be either virtual desktops

00:27:06.410 --> 00:27:08.960
or Windows servers that
are publishing apps.

00:27:08.960 --> 00:27:10.820
And because of that,
scalability of these

00:27:10.820 --> 00:27:15.440
plays a major part in
looking at your design.

00:27:15.440 --> 00:27:17.290
For VDI, it's really simple.

00:27:17.290 --> 00:27:19.850
It's kind of picking out the
best instance type that you

00:27:19.850 --> 00:27:21.740
might have for your workload.

00:27:21.740 --> 00:27:23.870
For server based
computing then, we

00:27:23.870 --> 00:27:27.890
start to want to look at single
instance scalability within GCP

00:27:27.890 --> 00:27:33.030
to understand how many users
can we get on a given workload.

00:27:33.030 --> 00:27:36.440
And Citrix did last year some
baseline scalability testing.

00:27:36.440 --> 00:27:40.160
We used Login VSI and we worked
through several iterations

00:27:40.160 --> 00:27:42.620
of various instance
types in GCP.

00:27:42.620 --> 00:27:45.800
And what we found at that time
was the best instance type

00:27:45.800 --> 00:27:48.530
that we had or instance size
that we had was the N1 standard

00:27:48.530 --> 00:27:51.920
8 for the workloads,
[INAUDIBLE] app workloads,

00:27:51.920 --> 00:27:54.650
for server-based
computing workloads.

00:27:54.650 --> 00:28:00.170
And we ran about 33
users per VM on that

00:28:00.170 --> 00:28:06.510
using a standardized knowledge
workload in Login VSI.

00:28:06.510 --> 00:28:08.100
Other considerations here.

00:28:08.100 --> 00:28:12.810
You want to look at optimizing
your costs through discounts.

00:28:12.810 --> 00:28:15.630
So of course, if you're
familiar with GCP,

00:28:15.630 --> 00:28:17.490
you know that by
default something

00:28:17.490 --> 00:28:19.920
that GCP has that other cloud
providers don't have are

00:28:19.920 --> 00:28:21.750
the sustained use discounts.

00:28:21.750 --> 00:28:25.410
So taking a look at how you
discount your environment

00:28:25.410 --> 00:28:29.010
based on VMs running
over a period of time.

00:28:29.010 --> 00:28:30.720
And there's a good
amount of detail here

00:28:30.720 --> 00:28:33.190
that I'm not going to go into.

00:28:33.190 --> 00:28:35.610
But also the committed use
discount, which is more,

00:28:35.610 --> 00:28:37.080
I guess, a traditional
discounting

00:28:37.080 --> 00:28:39.090
you'll see across
cloud providers, where

00:28:39.090 --> 00:28:40.975
you have one and three
year commitments where

00:28:40.975 --> 00:28:43.350
you pay a certain amount and
then you run those workloads

00:28:43.350 --> 00:28:45.580
100% of the time.

00:28:45.580 --> 00:28:48.690
Certainly for some of
the control element

00:28:48.690 --> 00:28:51.585
workloads that you may put
in your resource location,

00:28:51.585 --> 00:28:53.460
this is a really good
idea to take a look at.

00:28:53.460 --> 00:28:56.920
Because you know they're going
to be running 100% of the time.

00:28:56.920 --> 00:29:00.210
But we also want to take a look
at it from a perspective of,

00:29:00.210 --> 00:29:02.520
what does it mean for
running workloads?

00:29:02.520 --> 00:29:05.190
And Jeff was good enough
to put this graph together

00:29:05.190 --> 00:29:08.280
for us, as we did some of
our architecture discussions,

00:29:08.280 --> 00:29:10.230
to kind of take a look
at the breakpoints

00:29:10.230 --> 00:29:13.110
here for sustained and
committed use discounts.

00:29:13.110 --> 00:29:15.270
So the green is the
standard sustained use

00:29:15.270 --> 00:29:18.420
and how it increases over time.

00:29:18.420 --> 00:29:20.880
The blue curve is a
three year committed use

00:29:20.880 --> 00:29:24.540
and the purple curve is
a one year committed use.

00:29:24.540 --> 00:29:27.330
So what you see here where
those lines intersect is really

00:29:27.330 --> 00:29:30.120
the point at which it
starts to make sense

00:29:30.120 --> 00:29:33.930
to consider those committed use
discounts for your workloads.

00:29:33.930 --> 00:29:40.680
For instance here, if
you're running your workload

00:29:40.680 --> 00:29:44.510
for 50% of the
time over a month,

00:29:44.510 --> 00:29:46.260
then you want to start
to consider looking

00:29:46.260 --> 00:29:49.960
at committed use discounts.

00:29:49.960 --> 00:29:52.450
The other thing to consider
for those workloads that

00:29:52.450 --> 00:29:55.750
aren't going to be
using committed use

00:29:55.750 --> 00:29:57.530
is our autoscale technology.

00:29:57.530 --> 00:29:59.050
And this is a
technology that Citrix

00:29:59.050 --> 00:30:01.330
has developed within
the product that

00:30:01.330 --> 00:30:06.010
allows us to take our
workloads and dynamically power

00:30:06.010 --> 00:30:10.220
manage them based on
schedule and load.

00:30:10.220 --> 00:30:13.840
So we can define within
our configurations

00:30:13.840 --> 00:30:20.410
within Citrix when machines
should be started up

00:30:20.410 --> 00:30:21.920
based on a schedule.

00:30:21.920 --> 00:30:24.700
So Monday to Friday
8:00 to 5:00 you

00:30:24.700 --> 00:30:27.100
should have x number
of machines running.

00:30:27.100 --> 00:30:29.380
Overnight you should
have x minus 5,

00:30:29.380 --> 00:30:32.000
on weekends you should
have x minus 10.

00:30:32.000 --> 00:30:35.710
And we can also then bundle
that in with load-based

00:30:35.710 --> 00:30:38.703
for our server-based computing
and say when our load exceeds

00:30:38.703 --> 00:30:40.870
a certain point, then I'm
going to start to scale up

00:30:40.870 --> 00:30:41.703
additional machines.

00:30:46.720 --> 00:30:49.150
Finally, the Citrix ADC on GCP.

00:30:49.150 --> 00:30:52.210
So this is our Application
Delivery Controller.

00:30:52.210 --> 00:30:54.610
And that is what we
used to call NetScaler.

00:30:54.610 --> 00:30:56.682
Names have changed recently.

00:30:56.682 --> 00:30:59.140
The functionality that we have
in that that we're primarily

00:30:59.140 --> 00:31:01.300
looking at when we're
doing Citrix on GCP

00:31:01.300 --> 00:31:04.420
are using it as an ICA
proxy gateway as opposed

00:31:04.420 --> 00:31:05.860
to using the gateway services.

00:31:05.860 --> 00:31:07.570
And that's when
customers want to bring

00:31:07.570 --> 00:31:09.888
their own routing into play.

00:31:09.888 --> 00:31:11.930
So they don't want to
route through the internet.

00:31:11.930 --> 00:31:15.970
They want to route in and out
of their GCP resource locations

00:31:15.970 --> 00:31:18.430
individually to control traffic.

00:31:18.430 --> 00:31:20.570
As well as load
balancing for components.

00:31:20.570 --> 00:31:22.870
If we put StoreFront
in place, for instance.

00:31:22.870 --> 00:31:25.810
And potentially content
switching, as well.

00:31:25.810 --> 00:31:28.810
We do support single and multi
NIC configurations in that.

00:31:28.810 --> 00:31:31.960
So we can define our topology
from a network perspective

00:31:31.960 --> 00:31:34.710
and understand how our
communications are going

00:31:34.710 --> 00:31:37.690
to happen within our VNets.

00:31:37.690 --> 00:31:40.660
And we can deploy it today
as standalone instances.

00:31:40.660 --> 00:31:46.720
They're not yet
available in the store.

00:31:46.720 --> 00:31:50.260
So we have them as a download
and then there's a step

00:31:50.260 --> 00:31:53.340
by step deployment,
the URL here,

00:31:53.340 --> 00:31:55.090
as well as some
Deployment Manager scripts

00:31:55.090 --> 00:31:58.220
to get those going.

00:31:58.220 --> 00:32:00.320
On the horizon what
we're looking at really

00:32:00.320 --> 00:32:01.778
these are things
that we're looking

00:32:01.778 --> 00:32:04.640
at coming out with this
year and into next year.

00:32:04.640 --> 00:32:07.910
Machine Creation Services.

00:32:07.910 --> 00:32:10.250
Right now we can power
manage machines within GCP.

00:32:10.250 --> 00:32:13.010
But what we want to
do is get into being

00:32:13.010 --> 00:32:15.470
able to have a single instance
management across a number

00:32:15.470 --> 00:32:19.070
of machines, so we can manage
a single OS application package

00:32:19.070 --> 00:32:21.980
and then deploy it
to multiple machines.

00:32:21.980 --> 00:32:27.950
Having native Google ID
support in Citrix Workspace.

00:32:27.950 --> 00:32:30.290
So rather than having to
log on with Active Directory

00:32:30.290 --> 00:32:33.620
credentials, being able to log
on directly with your Google

00:32:33.620 --> 00:32:35.180
ID.

00:32:35.180 --> 00:32:36.822
As well as SD-WAN on GCP.

00:32:36.822 --> 00:32:38.780
And there was an announcement
today about that.

00:32:38.780 --> 00:32:43.520
SD-WAN on GCP will be available
within the store coming up

00:32:43.520 --> 00:32:44.960
in one month.

00:32:44.960 --> 00:32:50.570
And SD-WAN is used for
software-defined networking

00:32:50.570 --> 00:32:54.560
and setting up paths
between branches and data

00:32:54.560 --> 00:32:56.960
centers having multiple
redundant paths and logic

00:32:56.960 --> 00:32:58.980
and how you switch between them.

00:32:58.980 --> 00:33:01.970
And as well on the ADC side,
we have some enhancements

00:33:01.970 --> 00:33:03.980
coming up, primarily around HA.

00:33:03.980 --> 00:33:08.150
Having multiple ADCs and
being able to manage them.

00:33:08.150 --> 00:33:11.600
As well as, again, integrating
those into the marketplace.

00:33:11.600 --> 00:33:13.580
So with that, let me
turn it back over to Jeff

00:33:13.580 --> 00:33:18.448
and we'll do a walk through
of the reference architecture.

00:33:18.448 --> 00:33:19.490
JEFF ALLEN: Thanks, Rich.

00:33:19.490 --> 00:33:22.183
So what we're going to
do, take these pieces

00:33:22.183 --> 00:33:23.600
of the architecture
that Rich just

00:33:23.600 --> 00:33:33.155
told us about, the Cloud
Connectors, the VDA, et cetera.

00:33:33.155 --> 00:33:34.280
And we're going to explore.

00:33:34.280 --> 00:33:36.740
Basically this is kind of a
simple reference architecture,

00:33:36.740 --> 00:33:40.300
similar to actually what's
being built by the scripts.

00:33:40.300 --> 00:33:43.370
And we're going to look
at a couple of workflows

00:33:43.370 --> 00:33:46.190
to explore what each
architectural component is

00:33:46.190 --> 00:33:47.445
doing in those workflows.

00:33:47.445 --> 00:33:49.070
So the first one
we're going to look at

00:33:49.070 --> 00:33:53.630
is what happens when a user
logs in to the Citrix Workspace.

00:33:53.630 --> 00:33:56.370
So it starts on
the user's device,

00:33:56.370 --> 00:34:00.500
whether it's a laptop,
tablet, phone, whatever.

00:34:00.500 --> 00:34:02.210
For whatever operating
system, there

00:34:02.210 --> 00:34:04.580
are native Citrix
Workspace apps.

00:34:04.580 --> 00:34:07.940
There's also an
HTML5 Workspace app

00:34:07.940 --> 00:34:10.620
that you can access
in the browser.

00:34:10.620 --> 00:34:14.929
And so the user will access the
Citrix Workspace and log in.

00:34:14.929 --> 00:34:17.949
Now that log in request
goes up to Citrix Cloud.

00:34:17.949 --> 00:34:20.389
Citrix Cloud doesn't
authenticate the user directly.

00:34:20.389 --> 00:34:22.820
Citrix Cloud actually
delegates that authentication

00:34:22.820 --> 00:34:25.040
to the Active Directory
that's installed

00:34:25.040 --> 00:34:27.600
in the GCP environment.

00:34:27.600 --> 00:34:30.590
And so it does that by
communicating with those Cloud

00:34:30.590 --> 00:34:31.489
Connectors.

00:34:31.489 --> 00:34:33.469
It passes along that
authentication request

00:34:33.469 --> 00:34:35.900
through the Cloud
Connectors, which actually

00:34:35.900 --> 00:34:39.139
then authenticate the user
against the Active Directory

00:34:39.139 --> 00:34:41.199
domain controllers.

00:34:41.199 --> 00:34:43.090
Those Active Directory
domain controllers

00:34:43.090 --> 00:34:44.780
may be standalone
domain controllers,

00:34:44.780 --> 00:34:46.100
like they are in
the demo environment

00:34:46.100 --> 00:34:47.350
that our scripts are building.

00:34:47.350 --> 00:34:49.969
But more often than
not, it's some kind

00:34:49.969 --> 00:34:53.747
of maybe a replication scenario
where an on-prem Active

00:34:53.747 --> 00:34:55.580
Directory is being
replicated into the cloud

00:34:55.580 --> 00:34:57.913
or there's some kind of trust
relationship set up there.

00:34:57.913 --> 00:35:00.020
But once those
domain controllers

00:35:00.020 --> 00:35:02.660
authenticate the user,
they'll pass back

00:35:02.660 --> 00:35:05.610
through Cloud Connector,
the authentication results.

00:35:05.610 --> 00:35:08.960
Citrix Cloud will then say,
OK, I know who this user is.

00:35:08.960 --> 00:35:12.390
Now Citrix Cloud makes an
authorization decision.

00:35:12.390 --> 00:35:13.853
What does that user
have access to?

00:35:13.853 --> 00:35:15.770
That's part of the control
plane functionality

00:35:15.770 --> 00:35:17.630
that exists in Citrus Cloud.

00:35:17.630 --> 00:35:20.360
So it'll decide what virtual
apps, what virtual desktops

00:35:20.360 --> 00:35:22.410
does that user have access to.

00:35:22.410 --> 00:35:25.480
And then it will
render in the Workspace

00:35:25.480 --> 00:35:27.860
a menu that allows
the user to choose

00:35:27.860 --> 00:35:31.490
from one of the apps or desktops
that they have access to.

00:35:35.030 --> 00:35:37.580
So then the next workflow
we want to look at

00:35:37.580 --> 00:35:40.630
is what happens then when a
user chooses a virtual app

00:35:40.630 --> 00:35:42.710
and how does that
app get launched

00:35:42.710 --> 00:35:45.020
and how does that work.

00:35:45.020 --> 00:35:46.820
So again, we're starting here.

00:35:46.820 --> 00:35:49.100
The user is going to
say, click on Notepad.

00:35:49.100 --> 00:35:52.940
I want to run Notepad
as a virtual app.

00:35:52.940 --> 00:35:55.790
So that requests will go
up through Citrix Cloud.

00:35:55.790 --> 00:35:58.815
And Citrix Cloud is going
to then make a decision.

00:35:58.815 --> 00:36:00.440
There's a load
balancing decision here.

00:36:00.440 --> 00:36:02.510
Because you know,
this is a fleet

00:36:02.510 --> 00:36:08.140
of servers being used
by a group of users.

00:36:08.140 --> 00:36:09.730
And we have to
allocate a session

00:36:09.730 --> 00:36:10.730
on one of those servers.

00:36:10.730 --> 00:36:11.730
These are the VDAs.

00:36:11.730 --> 00:36:13.760
We have to make an
allocation decision.

00:36:13.760 --> 00:36:16.193
This is a session brokering
and management capabilities

00:36:16.193 --> 00:36:16.860
of Citrix Cloud.

00:36:16.860 --> 00:36:18.000
So we'll say, OK.

00:36:18.000 --> 00:36:19.790
Well this Notepad
session, I'm going

00:36:19.790 --> 00:36:23.360
to allocate that on one of
my available VDA servers.

00:36:23.360 --> 00:36:25.760
Now it's going to pass
that session initialization

00:36:25.760 --> 00:36:30.290
information into the VDA, again,
through the Cloud Connectors.

00:36:30.290 --> 00:36:32.870
And then Cloud Connector will
pass that along to the VDA.

00:36:32.870 --> 00:36:36.830
The VDA will actually
then log in as the user,

00:36:36.830 --> 00:36:45.040
launch Notepad, and then
initialize that user's

00:36:45.040 --> 00:36:46.420
virtual app session.

00:36:46.420 --> 00:36:50.420
This is the ICA traffic, which
we talked about, ICA proxies.

00:36:50.420 --> 00:36:53.110
Now by default,
the ICA traffic is

00:36:53.110 --> 00:36:56.680
going to stream
from the VDA server

00:36:56.680 --> 00:36:59.320
directly to Citrix Cloud
if there's connectivity

00:36:59.320 --> 00:37:01.140
between the VDA
and Citrix Cloud.

00:37:01.140 --> 00:37:03.640
It's going to go through the
gateway service of Citrix Cloud

00:37:03.640 --> 00:37:05.470
and then back to
the end user device.

00:37:05.470 --> 00:37:09.530
Now if the VDA can't communicate
directly with Citrix Cloud,

00:37:09.530 --> 00:37:12.070
it'll proxy that traffic
through the Cloud Connectors.

00:37:12.070 --> 00:37:15.530
So there's some path
decisions being made here.

00:37:15.530 --> 00:37:18.550
But assuming that that VDA can
communicate with Citrix Cloud,

00:37:18.550 --> 00:37:21.008
it's going to stream that ICA
traffic through the gateway

00:37:21.008 --> 00:37:23.050
service of Citrix Cloud
and then back to that end

00:37:23.050 --> 00:37:25.690
user who then is
going to see Notepad

00:37:25.690 --> 00:37:27.200
running on their device.

00:37:27.200 --> 00:37:30.760
And that's basically what we'll
demo at the end of the session.

00:37:33.340 --> 00:37:35.680
Now that was a quick
walk through of sort

00:37:35.680 --> 00:37:37.480
of a reference architecture.

00:37:37.480 --> 00:37:39.560
And now Rick is
going to join me.

00:37:39.560 --> 00:37:42.580
And we're going to actually walk
through a couple of real world

00:37:42.580 --> 00:37:43.720
architectures.

00:37:43.720 --> 00:37:45.130
Maybe how we adapted them.

00:37:45.130 --> 00:37:47.063
How they're a little
bit similar to,

00:37:47.063 --> 00:37:49.480
but in some ways different
than the reference architecture

00:37:49.480 --> 00:37:50.425
we just looked at.

00:37:50.425 --> 00:37:51.300
RICK DEHLINGER: Yeah.

00:37:51.300 --> 00:37:55.180
So one thing that you'll find
about Citrix technologies

00:37:55.180 --> 00:37:57.850
and about Citrix Cloud is that
they're cloud services, right?

00:37:57.850 --> 00:38:00.320
So there's a lot of flexibility
in what you can consume,

00:38:00.320 --> 00:38:02.070
how you want to consume it.

00:38:02.070 --> 00:38:06.790
We can leverage technologies
to tie the services together

00:38:06.790 --> 00:38:08.230
from an automation standpoint.

00:38:08.230 --> 00:38:09.940
Build those services.

00:38:09.940 --> 00:38:13.465
So you actually have a lot of
flexibility in what you can do

00:38:13.465 --> 00:38:15.935
and what you can
actually build with it.

00:38:15.935 --> 00:38:17.560
So I'll give it a
little bit of a pitch

00:38:17.560 --> 00:38:20.050
to come down and check out the
