when they're not in class

00:40:39.010 --> 00:40:41.255
and they paid for
them all up front.

00:40:41.255 --> 00:40:42.880
Then of course, over
time, those things

00:40:42.880 --> 00:40:45.050
become antiquated and
need to be replaced,

00:40:45.050 --> 00:40:50.570
which was the driving force
behind this engagement with us.

00:40:50.570 --> 00:40:53.210
So what they ultimately
ended up doing

00:40:53.210 --> 00:40:57.440
was leveraging GCP, our Citrix
Virtual Apps and Desktops,

00:40:57.440 --> 00:41:01.160
and also leveraging
Nvidia's GPO instances

00:41:01.160 --> 00:41:03.680
that you can run out on GCP.

00:41:03.680 --> 00:41:09.590
So they're now running these
apps out of a GCP location

00:41:09.590 --> 00:41:11.870
near.

00:41:11.870 --> 00:41:20.120
They're paying for their
consumption of both GPU

00:41:20.120 --> 00:41:22.920
and compute based on usage.

00:41:22.920 --> 00:41:25.250
So it ends up being
substantially less expensive

00:41:25.250 --> 00:41:27.110
for them.

00:41:27.110 --> 00:41:29.720
And they're now able to
logistically just have

00:41:29.720 --> 00:41:31.310
the students use
whatever device they

00:41:31.310 --> 00:41:34.640
happen to bring in
and run those things.

00:41:34.640 --> 00:41:38.330
So real quickly, so I leave
some room for Jeff here,

00:41:38.330 --> 00:41:40.592
the architecture that was
deployed for these guys

00:41:40.592 --> 00:41:42.050
actually is very
similar to the one

00:41:42.050 --> 00:41:44.810
that you see popping out the
back end of this automation

00:41:44.810 --> 00:41:46.940
tooling.

00:41:46.940 --> 00:41:49.267
So it was all very similar.

00:41:49.267 --> 00:41:51.350
The one difference from
the reference architecture

00:41:51.350 --> 00:41:53.367
that Jeff shared
with you earlier

00:41:53.367 --> 00:41:54.950
is that the instances,
instead of just

00:41:54.950 --> 00:41:56.960
being the general
purpose instances,

00:41:56.960 --> 00:41:59.030
they're actually
instances with Nvidia

00:41:59.030 --> 00:42:03.150
GPUs and their virtual vDWS.

00:42:03.150 --> 00:42:06.770
I always stumble on what
that acronym stands for.

00:42:06.770 --> 00:42:09.110
But they're basically
GPU accelerated instances

00:42:09.110 --> 00:42:13.850
that run up inside of GCP.

00:42:13.850 --> 00:42:16.010
They're also leveraging
Citrix Gateways

00:42:16.010 --> 00:42:17.660
at the perimeter of GCP.

00:42:17.660 --> 00:42:19.670
And the reason they did
that is because they

00:42:19.670 --> 00:42:22.760
wanted to have the absolute
best possible performance.

00:42:22.760 --> 00:42:28.680
So they got their users closest
to the nearest GCP region.

00:42:28.680 --> 00:42:31.680
They've got their
gateways there as well.

00:42:31.680 --> 00:42:34.760
So they've got the
utmost of performance

00:42:34.760 --> 00:42:36.960
of the pipe, the lowest
possible latency.

00:42:36.960 --> 00:42:41.090
And they've also got the
fullest featured protocol

00:42:41.090 --> 00:42:43.493
stack in the midst of it.

00:42:43.493 --> 00:42:45.660
So with that, I'm going to
go ahead and hand it over

00:42:45.660 --> 00:42:48.400
to this last one.

00:42:48.400 --> 00:42:50.670
RICH MEESTERS: So this
customer, this next customer,

00:42:50.670 --> 00:42:53.580
was a public sector
organization with a mission

00:42:53.580 --> 00:42:57.100
critical multi-tier app.

00:42:57.100 --> 00:42:59.220
Some of the challenges.

00:42:59.220 --> 00:43:03.360
They had heavy
database interactions.

00:43:03.360 --> 00:43:05.200
They are trying to
move to the cloud,

00:43:05.200 --> 00:43:06.950
but they're having
trouble because if they

00:43:06.950 --> 00:43:09.330
move the application
away from the database,

00:43:09.330 --> 00:43:11.063
they weren't getting
great performance.

00:43:11.063 --> 00:43:12.480
They have a
distributed workforce.

00:43:12.480 --> 00:43:17.430
So around the country, they only
get to access this application.

00:43:17.430 --> 00:43:20.660
And they weren't getting
consistent performance.

00:43:20.660 --> 00:43:24.870
And they'd also invested
in an on-prem multi-factor

00:43:24.870 --> 00:43:27.030
authentication scheme
using smart cards.

00:43:27.030 --> 00:43:30.150
And even in the
cloud, they needed

00:43:30.150 --> 00:43:32.730
to authenticate their users
against this smart card

00:43:32.730 --> 00:43:34.090
infrastructure.

00:43:34.090 --> 00:43:38.010
So the solution was to move the
application and the database

00:43:38.010 --> 00:43:40.680
into Google Cloud so that
the chatty application was

00:43:40.680 --> 00:43:42.960
close to the database
in Google Cloud,

00:43:42.960 --> 00:43:46.230
leveraging the Google global
network for distributing

00:43:46.230 --> 00:43:48.750
this application using
Citrix and virtual apps

00:43:48.750 --> 00:43:50.970
to their distributed workforce.

00:43:50.970 --> 00:43:53.820
And the unique thing
about our configuration

00:43:53.820 --> 00:43:56.070
was that we set up
federated authentication

00:43:56.070 --> 00:43:58.780
against that on-prem
identity provider.

00:43:58.780 --> 00:44:01.410
So they could leverage their
existing infrastructure

00:44:01.410 --> 00:44:03.400
to authenticate users.

00:44:03.400 --> 00:44:08.430
So again, it's similar to
the architecture we already

00:44:08.430 --> 00:44:09.210
looked at.

00:44:09.210 --> 00:44:10.540
A couple of differences.

00:44:10.540 --> 00:44:14.700
One, you'll notice that the
client devices don't initially

00:44:14.700 --> 00:44:17.780
talk to the Citrix Cloud
when they authenticate users.

00:44:17.780 --> 00:44:19.140
Now they're configured.

00:44:19.140 --> 00:44:23.280
The gateway is actually
running on an appliance in GCP.

00:44:23.280 --> 00:44:25.050
And this gives you a
lot more flexibility

00:44:25.050 --> 00:44:27.490
to how you configure
that appliance.

00:44:27.490 --> 00:44:30.390
Here it's configured
not to authenticate

00:44:30.390 --> 00:44:32.970
against the local Active
Directory domain controller,

00:44:32.970 --> 00:44:35.850
but instead using
SAML to use that

00:44:35.850 --> 00:44:38.250
on prem identity provider,
which allowed this customer

00:44:38.250 --> 00:44:43.020
to leverage the smart cards
to do their authentication.

00:44:43.020 --> 00:44:45.660
And then once the users
are authenticated,

00:44:45.660 --> 00:44:49.750
then the workflow is similar
to what we looked at earlier.

00:44:53.630 --> 00:44:54.170
All right.

00:44:54.170 --> 00:44:55.980
So we've got a few minutes left.

